WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:06.160
I'm Bob Sutton. I'm an organizational&nbsp;
psychologist I've been at the Stanford&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:12.400
engineering school for more than 35 years studied&nbsp;
all sorts of elements of leading innovation. How I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:17.600
see myself as at that sort of uncomfortable&nbsp;
intersection between the rigor of academia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:23.520
and what it takes to get things done in the real&nbsp;
world. So in "Leading Innovation" I'm bringing you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:29.840
ideas at the intersection between three things.&nbsp;
First of all the best research we could find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:35.280
about what leaders do to promote innovation&nbsp;
things like creating constructive conflict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:40.800
psychological safety getting people to come up&nbsp;
with a wide range of ideas and then to pick the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:46.640
best. The second part is to give you some of&nbsp;
the best case studies we can find from Apple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:53.040
IDEO, Pixar, Intel so you can learn from&nbsp;
the best practices and avoid the worst.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:58.960
And then finally we bring you veteran executives&nbsp;
so you can learn from Perry Klebahn who invented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:04.480
the modern snowshoe now sort of a master&nbsp;
innovation teacher. "Innovation is something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:11.840
the uncertainty can drive fear." Carl Liebert was&nbsp;
CEO of the largest residential real estate company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:17.600
"change before you have to change once you get&nbsp;
behind it's really difficult to spend enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:24.000
money to catch up." Sylvia Acevedo her last job&nbsp;
was CEO of the girl scouts before that she was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:31.120
a rocket scientist a tech executive. "When people&nbsp;
didn't look for somebody like me I would focus on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:38.160
the skills and then that allowed me in." Finally&nbsp;
the remarkable and imaginative venture capitalist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:44.160
Michael Dearing. "You have to tell a story you&nbsp;
have to be great at making sense of the complex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:50.880
reality for people." So in "Leading Innovation" by&nbsp;
bringing you through a tour of those three things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:56.880
punctuated by all sorts of different exercises is&nbsp;
to help you understand the cases and companies you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:03.760
can learn from the people I hope who can inspire&nbsp;
you and always we go back to what we think is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:13.600
best evidence about what good leaders do and how&nbsp;
they design their teams and their organizations.

